# Every dot-named path component this repository tracks, one per line.
#
# WHY AN ALLOWLIST AND NOT A DENYLIST. Development tooling keeps its local
# configuration in a dot-directory at the repository root. Those directories
# are not ours, they are not in .gitignore, and a single `git add .` commits
# one. This repository is public, so what lands there is published.
#
# A denylist can only refuse the tool someone already thought of, and writing
# a tool's directory name into this repository is itself the thing being
# avoided. An allowlist refuses everything nobody has vouched for, names none
# of them, and keeps working for tools that do not exist yet.
#
# ADDING A LINE IS A DECISION, not a way to make the gate quiet. A new entry
# says: this dot-entry belongs in version control, someone looked at it, and
# it carries nothing local, personal or machine-specific.
#
# Removing a tracked dot-entry means removing its line too. An entry matching
# nothing is a stale excuse, and the check fails on it for the same reason it
# fails on an unknown one.

.editorconfig
.github
.gitignore
.yamllint.yml
